Thank you — these targets sound useful, and the stated limitations are appropriately scoped.

Please use a top-level fuzz/ directory rather than tests/. The existing tests/ tree is for deterministic conformance and QEMU checks, while libFuzzer targets, seed corpora, and dictionaries have a distinct toolchain and execution model.

A draft PR against current main would be welcome. Please include:
- the three target groups and their dictionaries/seeds;
- a short fuzz/README with exact build/run commands and required cargo-fuzz/toolchain versions;
- bounded smoke commands suitable for maintainer reproduction;
- no generated crash artifacts or oversized evolved corpus; and
- the pinned upstream commit and latest run summary in the PR description.

Keeping the related harness plumbing in one draft PR is reasonable because it shares one fuzz workspace and review setup. We can split targets later only if review shows materially independent build or dependency concerns.
